schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Three kinds of trust: competence, integrity, benevolence"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A three-part model for the trust a community finance system needs:
    competence trust ("can you do what you say?"), integrity trust ("will
    you do what you said?", built through consistency), and benevolence
    trust ("do you care about me?", built by listening and helping without
    keeping score).
  source_traditions:
    - organizational-trust research (the competence/integrity/benevolence typology is a standard framing in trust literature, applied here to community finance specifically)
    - Francis Fukuyama's social-trust-and-cooperation thesis (cited elsewhere in the same article)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used to argue that community finance needs all three at once: competence
    to manage funds, integrity to follow the rules, and benevolence so
    members believe the rules protect them rather than trap them. Each type
    is paired with a concrete building practice (e.g. "I am still learning
    this" earning more competence-trust than overclaiming).
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "'trust' used loosely as a single undifferentiated feeling, without the article's three-part decomposition"
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      competence trust is about ability, integrity trust is about kept
      promises, benevolence trust is about care for the other person's
      interests — the article treats these as separable and each
      independently buildable.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "trust" as one thing collapses the article's practical
    distinction between three different failure modes (incompetence,
    unreliability, self-interest) that require different remedies.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-trust.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-06b003d4c0b2
  source_lineage: building-trust.md
